        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for clarification!\r\n\r\nI am strongly against instant feedback. Mostly because it gets annoying pretty quickly at the rate 'mini.ai' and 'mini.surround' keys are expected to be used.\r\n\r\n> 2\\. If I happen to finish pressing a key slightly over one second, the feedback message will flash briefly, causing the screen to flicker.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, this will be the case for any manually set finite delay. Yes, users can tweak it to adjust to their typing speed, yet my instinct is that 1 second delay should cover majority frequently used textobjects/surroundings. Mostly because they are expected to be typed quicker as muscle memory settles in.\r\n\r\n> Moreover, when I first started using mini, I mistakenly thought mini was so slow that I needed to wait one second for feedback (I didn’t read the help file carefully).\r\n\r\n*This* is a real concern. I remember seeing couple of such feedbacks. The main point of these helper messages were exactly for the beginners who are not yet comfortable with using textobjects and surroundings to remind them what should be done after pressing `a`, `i`, `sd`, `sr`, etc. Probably making it documented more prominently is a good idea. I'll take a look.",
